WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:04.030
Marketing, supply chain and finance each own their data.

2
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:06.730
One central team cannot keep up with all of them.

3
00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:08.590
It becomes a bottleneck.

4
00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:12.350
But if every team does its own thing, nothing fits together.

5
00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:13.750
The answer is in the middle.

6
00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:16.300
It is called a data mesh.

7
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.085
Think of a shopping mall.

8
00:00:18.105 --> 00:00:19.560
Each shop runs itself.

9
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:21.895
It knows its own products best.

10
00:00:21.915 --> 00:00:26.355
But the mall gives every shop the same building, power and safety rules.

11
00:00:26.375 --> 00:00:29.418
In a mesh, each domain team owns its data products.

12
00:00:29.438 --> 00:00:30.965
That is the shop.

13
00:00:30.985 --> 00:00:33.802
A central team owns the standards and the platform.

14
00:00:33.822 --> 00:00:35.840
That is the mall.

15
00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:38.625
On Google Cloud, five blocks build it.

16
00:00:38.645 --> 00:00:40.262
Cloud Storage is the lake.

17
00:00:40.282 --> 00:00:41.938
BigQuery is the warehouse.

18
00:00:41.958 --> 00:00:44.585
Dataplex organizes and governs.

19
00:00:44.605 --> 00:00:46.610
The catalog makes data searchable.

20
00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:49.620
And Analytics Hub shares it, without copies.

21
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.107
One mapping to remember.

22
00:00:51.127 --> 00:00:53.605
A Dataplex lake is one domain.

23
00:00:53.625 --> 00:00:56.605
The trap: one team that owns every dataset,

24
00:00:56.625 --> 00:01:00.230
when the story describes independent domains.

25
00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:01.490
Recap.

26
00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:03.403
Domains own the data products.

27
00:01:03.423 --> 00:01:06.675
A central team owns the rules and the platform.

28
00:01:06.695 --> 00:01:07.762
Line three, done.

29
00:01:07.782 --> 00:01:10.520
Try the checkpoint below.
